From: "Barak A. Pearlmutter" <bap@debian.org>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 19:28:16 +0100
Subject: L1Constraint: always shrink radius by a few ULPs in ProximalStep
 retry

L1Constraint::ProximalStep() re-projects onto a slightly smaller ball
if floating-point error left the result outside the ball.  It detected
"no progress" with `newLambda == eT(lambda)`, but with excess precision
(x87 on i386) that comparison happens in extended precision and sees a
difference that vanishes when newLambda is stored.  The recursion then
never makes progress and overflows the stack, causing SIGSEGV in
L1ConstraintProjectionTest and FASTAWoodFunctionTest on i386.

Instead of testing for progress, always subtract at least a few ULPs of
eT.  Rounding is monotonic, so the stored radius is then strictly
smaller regardless of excess precision.  This also fixes the old
fallback for fmat, where eT(nexttoward(lambda, 0.0)) rounds back to
eT(lambda) and so removed nothing.
---
 include/ensmallen_bits/fbs/l1_constraint_impl.hpp | 18 ++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/include/ensmallen_bits/fbs/l1_constraint_impl.hpp b/include/ensmallen_bits/fbs/l1_constraint_impl.hpp
index aff3ed2..19b3e57 100644
--- a/include/ensmallen_bits/fbs/l1_constraint_impl.hpp
+++ b/include/ensmallen_bits/fbs/l1_constraint_impl.hpp
@@ -153,14 +153,16 @@ void L1Constraint::ProximalStep(MatType& coordinates,
   const eT newNorm = norm(coordinates, 1);
   if (newNorm > eT(lambda) && eT(lambda) > eT(0))
   {
-    // Shrink the L1 ball by the amount of the error.
-    eT newLambda = (eT(lambda) - 2 * (newNorm - eT(lambda)));
-    if (newLambda == eT(lambda))
-    {
-      // Make sure we at least remove a few ULPs.
-      newLambda = eT(lambda) -
-          5 * (eT(lambda) - eT(std::nexttoward(lambda, 0.0)));
-    }
+    // Shrink the L1 ball by twice the error, but always by at least a few
+    // ULPs of eT.  We can't test whether we made progress: with excess
+    // precision (x87 on i386) a comparison can see a difference that
+    // vanishes on storage.  Since rounding is monotonic, subtracting at
+    // least a few ULPs guarantees the stored radius is strictly smaller.
+    const eT minShrink = 4 * eT(std::max(
+        (double) std::numeric_limits<eT>::epsilon(),
+        (double) std::numeric_limits<double>::epsilon())) * eT(lambda);
+    const eT newLambda = eT(lambda) -
+        std::max(eT(2 * (newNorm - eT(lambda))), minShrink);
 
     L1Constraint newConstraint(newLambda);
     newConstraint.ProximalStep(coordinates, 0.0 /* ignored */);
